--TEST--
The program the primer annotates runs as written: each binding names its type once, a member's optional knobs are one trailing anonymous object, and `as` is the only conversion (`rule:types/declaration`, `rule:core-api/options-bag`, `rule:types/conversion`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:programs/a-complete-program-annotated
// The primer's own program, so the output printed beside it in the chapter is
// pinned here rather than only read.
array<string> $amounts = ["3", "11", "7"];
array<string> $ordered = Core\Arr::sort($amounts, {order: Core\Order::Asc});

int $total = 0;
foreach ($ordered as string $each) {
    $total = $total + ($each as int);
}

echo Core\Json::encode($ordered), " ", $total, "\n";

// `rule:core-api/options-bag`: the knobs are keys in one trailing anonymous object, so
// the other order is the same call with one key written differently.
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Arr::sort($amounts, {order: Core\Order::Desc})), "\n";

// The object is optional, and leaving it out is the member's own default —
// which has to agree with the bag that names that default outright.
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Arr::sort($amounts)), "\n";

// `rule:types/conversion`: `as` produces the type asked for or throws. It
// never guesses a number out of text that is not one.
try {
    echo ("seven" as int), "\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}

// `rule:types/declaration`: a name is declared once and keeps that type. The
// loop above wrote to `$total` many times; it is still the `int` it was
// declared as, and arithmetic on it is `int` arithmetic.
echo $total * 2, "\n";
--EXPECT--
["11","3","7"] 21
["7","3","11"]
["11","3","7"]
cannot convert string "seven" to `int`
42
